msgid "This situation is called a **fail state**. A fail state occurs when `cin` encounters an unexpected input format. A boolean flag called `failbit` is set to indicate that `cin` is in a fail state. You can check if `cin` is in a fail state using the `fail()` method, which returns `true` if `cin` is in a fail state and `false` otherwise. For example:"
msgstr ""

msgid "When `cin` is in a fail state, it will not process any further input until the fail state is cleared. This means that if we try to read more input using `cin`, it will not work until we clear the fail state."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before we can read more input, we need to do two main things:"
msgstr ""

msgid "clear the fail state using the `clear()` method. `cin.clear();` will set the fail flag to `false`, and"
msgstr ""

msgid "ignore the characters in the input buffer that have wrong format using the `ignore()` method. `cin.ignore(<int n>, <char c>);` will ignore up to `n` characters in the input buffer or until the character `c` is encountered, whichever comes first."
msgstr ""

